                new (
                    "00TQx00000SIRrJMAX",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: New EU Multiannual Financial Framework (MFF) presented to the EP (2028-2034)",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2025-07-18").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: New EU Multiannual Financial Framework (MFF) presented to the EP (2028-2034)",
                        Description = "To: gln@unops.org\nCC: LaetitiaK@unops.org; JeromeDT@unops.org; FreyaVG@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: New EU Multiannual Financial Framework (MFF) presented to the EP (2028-2034)\nBody:\nDear colleagues,Last wednesday the European Commission presented its proposal for an ambitious and dynamic long-term EU budget, the so-called Multiannual Financial Framework (MFF), which will run for seven years, starting in 2028. Almost €2 trillion, or 1.26% of the average EU's gross national income between 2028 and 2034, will support Europe’s ambition to be independent, safe, prosperous and thrive over the coming decade.Europe faces an increasing number of challenges in numerous areas such as security, defence, competitiveness, migration, energy and climate resilience. These reflect systemic geopolitical and economic shifts that require a strong and forward-looking response.  The Commission therefore proposes a fundamental redesign of the EU budget, which will be more streamlined, flexible and impactful. It will significantly enhance the EU's capacity to deliver on core policies, while addressing new and emerging priorities. This budget will continue to support people, business, Member States, regions, partners, and - above all - the EU's collective future. While the modalities have been highly criticised by the European Parliament  and not only (apparently President Von der Leyen did not share the text until a few days ago and the text seems to have been drafted by a very tiny group of people) I thought it would be interesting to share with all of you the numbers that are on the table and that concern our work (external action).Heading 3 - Global Europe, which includes  the Common Foreign and Security Policy and Overseas Countries and Territories (including Greenland) amounts to EUR 215.2 billion.What we know for now on the geographic distribution is:Candidates/accession countries will receive €42 billion.The largest share is intended for Sub-Saharan African countries, with nearly €60 billion, North African and Middle Eastern countries, with €42 billion. These are the regions from which funds will be drawn to finance agreements with third countries aimed at curbing migrant departures or increasing returns. Ukraine is treated separately. Ursula von der Leyen has announced €100 billion for resilience and reconstruction. However, the resources are expected to remain outside the budget and flow into the “Ukraine Facility,” likely through a new loan program guaranteed by the EU.\n\nPlease note that this is still to be approved unanimously by the EU Council of Ministers and then to be given the consent by the European Parliament, but it gives you an idea on which direction the budget is taking.\nI will be happy to answer any question you may have\nBest regardsCarmen\nMariacarmen Colitti | Head of Office and Representative | Partnerships and Liaison Group | Brussels Liaison Office | Brussels, Belgium | Tel: + 32 2 7881840| www.unops.org \n\nKeep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube. \nSubscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
