                ),
                new (
                    "00TQx00000OlTf5MAF",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Notes from ED meeting with Danish State Secretary, Elsebeth Søndergaard Krone on May 2",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2025-05-05").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Notes from ED meeting with Danish State Secretary, Elsebeth Søndergaard Krone on May 2",
                        Description = "To: kirstined@unops.org; freyavg@unops.org; rahulm@unops.org; carolinapo@unops.org; naimoh@unops.org; jakobt@unops.org; marijev@unops.org; dalilag@unops.org; elenage@unops.org; timl@unops.org; massimodi@unops.org; eleneag@unops.org\nCC: eo.cph@unops.org; danielgi@unops.org; jorge.moreiradasilva@unops.org; kerriet@unops.org; oonaa@unops.org; hilaryb@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Notes from ED meeting with Danish State Secretary, Elsebeth Søndergaard Krone on May 2\nBody:\nOn May 2 the ED met with the newly appointed Danish State Secretary for Development, Ms. Elsebeth Søndergaard Krone who had formally assumed her position the day before. \nThe State Secretary accepted an invitation by the ED to soon visit the UN City and meet the UNCT Country team.\nThe State Secretary was pleased to engage in conversations on how to deal with the implementation gap - a topic that should be discussed at the upcoming Copenhagen Dialogue, tentatively planned for October. Although this was a topic of the greatest interest to the Danish government, the State Secretary pointed to the extraordinarily busy time with the Danish EU Presidency and membership of the Security Council.\nThe State Secretary thanked for the \"incredible\" work done on behalf of Denmark in Ukraine. She had already seen some of the projects and looked forward to seeing even more results on the ground. The ED pointed towards the potential for expanded collaboration in Africa, to which the State Secretary responded that fund allocations were done locally at the individual embassies in Africa, whereas in Ukraine it was done centrally from Copenhagen. She therefore urged local UNOPS colleagues to approach the respective Danish embassies. \nFinally the State Secretary looked very much forward to the gathering of the CEB. It would be a testament to the reestablished confidence in UNOPS - and the Danish government would continue to support UNOPS wherever possible.       \n***  The State Secretary initially assured the ED that the Danish government was committed to stick to 0.7% ODA in their new development aid strategy - despite internal pressure on shifting funds away from development towards military spending. The government was determined to invest in both peace and defence. \nThe ED complimented the Danish government's approach - noting that Denmark had been one of the few countries that has consistently and historically defended human rights, set ambitious climate targets and provided at least 0.7% of GNI in ODA. Also in light of this, it was positive that the CEB would gather in Denmark. The ED thanked for Denmark's support during the S3i crisis and for helping in getting the CEB to gather in Denmark. \nThe ED pointed towards UNOPS being able to showcase the very tangible results of ODA funding - especially in fragile and conflict affected areas. The ED also invited the State Secretary to visit the UN City, the humanitarian warehouse and to meet the UNCT Country Team. \nUpon inquiries from the State Secretary the ED explained the circumstances surrounding the S3i revelations, the comprehensive response plan, the ongoing transformational initiatives and the aspiration for a growing UNOPS as demand was increasing. \nThe State Secretary urged the ED to share UNOPS reform experiences with other UN entities - as it could serve as a role model for potential upcoming reforms. The State Secretary looked forward to upcoming dialogue about a broader reform of the UN in relation to UN80. Talks about reforming the UN had been ongoing for decades, but the US funding cut had created a burning platform which necessitated a more efficient UN with less overlapping mandates, better sharing of services, etc. It was a key message from the Danish government, that all UN agencies should stick to their respective mandates. \nThe ED informed about UNOPS next strategic plan for 2026-29 where the underlying theme was the need to scale up and speed up - which would also encompass doing things more efficiently. The ED also explained that UNOPS would be mission-driven, but not programmatic. It would mean that UNOPS should not accept all projects and be ready to deliver on the 8 missions. UNOPS would also move away from measuring output to outcomes. \nFinally the State Secretary pointed towards the fact that at every COP there was an enormous pressure for more funding. As a consequence far too many different funds had been set up - with great absorption difficulties. The process of implementation was far too slow and results on the ground were not visible fast enough. \nThe State Secretary considered if a sort of one-shop UN access point for delegating tasks might be a way of delegating tasks among UN entities - and avoiding overlapping tasks/mandates. \nIn confidence the State Secretary mentioned that she found that far too often UNDP had accepted projects that UNDP did not have the capacities to fulfill. \nBest,Asbjørn\n\n-- \n\nAsbjørn Brink| Head of Northern Europe Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Tel: +45 40 80 36 54 | Follow us on social media and subscribe to our newsletter. | www.unops.org",
