                        Description = "To: agnes.annells@fcdo.gov.uk\nCC: sally.clifford@fcdo.gov.uk; naimoh@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Documents for Executive Board Annual Session, June 2-5\nBody:\nDear Agnes, \nAs we are ramping up preparations for the Annual Session at the Executive Board on June 2-5, I wanted to provide you with a brief overview of the UNOPS relevant documents that have been shared and which are all available at the website.  \nOnce you have had a chance to glance over the topics for discussion, I was hoping that you might have time for a brief online, informal catch-up. Feel free to let me know when it might suit you. \nFor your ease of reference please find below a list of the informal meetings relevant to UNOPS. \nInformal meetings relevant to UNOPS (see full overview here)28 February: Briefing by UNOPS Internal Audit and Investigation Group (IAIG) on its annual workplan and consolidated risk assessment25 March: Briefing by UNOPS on the Process Innovation and Digitalization programme25 April: Joint workshop on the development of the Strategic Plans 2026-2029 of UNDP, UNFPA, UNOPS, UNICEF and UN-Women12 May: Informal consultation on:Report on UNOPS Internal Audit and Investigation activities in 2024, and management responseReport of the UNOPS Audit Advisory Committee, and management response Report of the UNOPS Ethics Office on 2024 activities, and management response13 May: Briefing on the UNOPS Strategic Plan, 2026-202916 May: Joint informal consultation on implementation efforts on the repositioning of the United Nations development system19 May: Pre-session informal consultation on the annual session 2025 of the Executive Board of UNDP, UNFPA, UNOPS20 May: Informal consultation on the Annual Report of the UNOPS Executive Director:Progress report on the implementation of the UNOPS Restated Strategic Plan 2022-2025UNOPS report on the recommendations of the Joint Inspection Unit in 202421 May: Joint informal consultation on protection against sexual exploitation and abuse and sexual harassment22 May: Joint informal consultation on addressing racism and racial discrimination28 May: Informal consultations on draft decisions29 May: Informal consultations on draft decisions29 May: Closed informal consultations with UNOPS on the:UNOPS Ethics office on its workplan for 2025Unredacted report of the external third-party review of the Comprehensive Response PlanExternal formative evaluation of the Process Innovation and Digitalization Programme (PID)30 May: Informal consultations on draft decisions2-5 June: Annual Session of the Executive Board \nDocuments for Annual SessionFor ease of reference - and completely on my shoulders - I have provided a very brief summary for some of the key documents - marked in italics below. \nEfforts on repositioning of the UNDSInformation note on UNOPS efforts on the repositioning of the UN Development SystemThe note shows how UNOPS actively supports the UNDS reform by helping make operations more efficient — think shared services and better coordination with Resident Coordinators. The next Strategic Plan also aims to ensure that UNOPS stay aligned with broader UN goals. Basically, UNOPS is staying practical and focused on getting things done to support the SDGs.\nInternal Audit and InvestigationAnnual report of the Internal Audit and Investigations Group on internal audit and investigation activities in 2024UNOPS’ governance and risk management were deemed “partially satisfactory,” indicating that while systems are in place, there’s room for improvement. IAIG conducted 15 internal audits and 54 project audits, issuing 140 recommendations. 96% of these recommendations were implemented, reflecting the strong management commitment. IAIG received 370 complaints (a 17% increase from 2023), opened 167 cases, and completed 144 investigations. A third-party review confirmed that UNOPS is mostly ready to adopt the new 2025 Global Internal Audit Standards, with some areas needing enhancement.Annual Report of the UNOPS Audit Advisory Committee for 2024The Committee emphasizes the need for stronger internal controls and risk management, particularly in financial oversight and ethics.UNOPS management response to the 2024 annual report of the Audit Advisory Committee\nEthicsActivities of the UNOPS Ethics Office in 2024The Ethics Office experienced a 22% rise in ethics-related matters, handling 1,582 cases, with about 60% related to services for personnel. Efforts continued to strengthen the whistleblower mechanism, focusing on completing reforms to rebuild trust internally and externally. The Office emphasizes training and awareness-raising activities to promote ethical standards and expected behavior among personnel.Information note on the UNOPS Management response to the 2024 Annual Report of the Ethics Office\nProtection against sexual exploitation and abuse and sexual harassmentJoint information note on the Protection against Sexual Exploitation and Abuse and Sexual Harassment\nAddressing racism and racial discriminationUpdates on the UNOPS efforts to implement JIU recommendations on racism and racial discriminationThe note shows how UNOPS is stepping up on inclusion — adding voluntary self-ID options, running anti-racism training, and making sure fair hiring and career growth are a priority. UNOPS is teaming up with other UN bodies and making accountability part of the plan. Basically: more awareness, more action, better culture.\nAnnual Report of the UNOPS Executive DirectorReport on the enhanced accountability framework of UNOPS + annexesThe report demonstrates how UNOPS has revamped the accountability framework to align with the latest UN standards, following a comprehensive review against the Joint Inspection Unit’s 2023 benchmarks. Key improvements include: ￼Clearer Roles: Defined responsibilities at all organizational levels to ensure transparency and effective decision-making; Ethical Conduct: Reinforced commitment to ethical behavior across the organization; Continuous Improvement: Plans to integrate lessons learned and best practices from within UNOPS and other UN entities. Starting in 2026, UNOPS will provide annual updates to the Executive Board on the progress of these accountability measures.Report on the implementation of the UNOPS Restated Strategic Plan, 2022-2025In 2024, UNOPS operated in over 130 countries and managed over 1,100 projects, totaling about $2.7 billion:Procurement: UNOPS handled $1.7 billion in procurement, sourcing goods and services from more than 5,800 suppliers worldwide. This played a big role in supporting partners’ operations on the ground.Grants: Over $380 million in grants were managed, supporting 1,700+ partners in implementing development projects.Human Resources: Managed contracts and salaries for nearly 9,600 personnel, helping to implement projects at the local level.Partnerships: In 2024, UNOPS signed agreements worth almost $4 billion.Annex 1: UNOPS contribution and management results frameworkAnnex 2: UNOPS framework for sustainable implementation approachesAnnex 3: UNOPS 2024 annual report on the recommendations of the Joint Inspection UnitAnnex 4: Internal oversight arrangementsSDG 1 Summary ReportSDG 11 Summary ReportCountry reports on UNOPS contributions for the GoalsCase studies of UNOPS contributions for the most vulnerable peopleUtilisation of the second tranche allotted for the implementation of the comprehensive response plan\n\nEnjoy the reading and bon weekend when you get there!\nAll the best,Asbjørn",
