ore concrete risks regarding UNOPS' operations in fragile and conflict affected places, shortage of talent, sexual abuse due to the geographical operations of UNOPS, regaining trust among partners since S3i failures, implementing the many reforms at UNOPS and increasing financial constraints.     \n\nOrganisational cultureThe informal information note provides an update on the implementation of the work plan to transform the organisational culture. Status of each action point is in this annex.    \n\nImplementation of the Comprehensive Response PlanThis is the last update to the EB on the implementation of the CRP. As the note was written in November, 3 of the outstanding recommendations were yet to be completed by the end of 2024. These related to 1) revising the management and organisational culture towards broader engagement and less top-down, 2) invest in outcome-reporting, and 3) revision of financial rules and regulations.  The EB is asked to release the third tranche (11.8M USD) for the implementation and to initiate the second independent review of the implementation of the CRP.  \n\nReview of the whistleblower processThe informal information note provides an overview of the now completed overhaul of UNOPS' whistleblower mechanism. The new system is set to launch in January 2025 and will introduce: 1) a central platform for reporting, 2) ressources to provide early informal guidance, 3) a workplace conduct team within PLG, 4) a new advisory group to address systemic issues. \n\nPortfolio risk assessment of UNOPSAs a response to the original KPMG recommendations a portfolio risk assessment is presented that outlines a number of risks in UNOPS' portfolio. These relate e.g. to 1) challenges in managing complex infrastructure projects, 2) large-scale procurement of health products, 3) capacity of supplivers and implementing partners. \n\nCalculation method for excess reservesThe note presents a suggestion for revising the calculation methodology for calculating UNOPS' excess reserves. The proposed changes are to ensure that only liquid assets are calculated as part of the potential excess reserves and that only if the excess reserve exceeds 20% of the minimum operational reserve, it should trigger the refund process. By the end of 2023 UNOPS distributed 123.2M USD of the excess reserve back to partners and all other funds had been transferred to the RC system and the SDG fund. Only 0.6M USD is withheld as these relate to discussions with partners. \n\nImplementation of the PIDThis is the annual status of implementation of the PID that points towards progress in seven programmes (country engagements, partnerships and project management, procurement, HR, finance, data, market research for the new ERP). Notice that the selection of a new ERP system can only be done after a formal evaluation is presented to the EB (as requested by the EB). Therefore the timeline for the PID has been extended by one year to 2028.I hope this brief overview helps - and I look very much forward to hearing your initial thinking about the topics. \nAll the best,Asbjørn-- \n\nAsbjørn Brink| Head of Northern Europe Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Tel: +45 40 80 36 54 | Follow us on social media and subscribe to our newsletter. | www.unops.org",
