                ),
                new (
                    "00TQx00000K7czgMAB",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Follow-up: UNOPS/FCDO in Kenya",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2025-01-09").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Follow-up: UNOPS/FCDO in Kenya",
                        Description = "To: sophia.chemei@fcdo.gov.uk\nCC: sharonle@unops.org; fumiea@unops.org; naimoh@unops.org; elenage@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Follow-up: UNOPS/FCDO in Kenya\nBody:\nDear Sophia,\nHappy 2025! \nIt was an absolute pleasure meeting you before the Christmas break! Thank you for taking the time to connect with us and share insights about the critical work you do in Kenya.\nWith our expertise in infrastructure, procurement, and fund management, UNOPS could be uniquely positioned to manage the FCDO initiative supporting Kenyan SMEs. Our already existing framework agreement with the UK streamlines the process for signing an agreement, eliminating the need for a lengthy tender process - should you chose UNOPS. This allows you to focus on strategic priorities rather than administrative tasks.\nSince 2010, UNOPS has successfully delivered Fund Management services on behalf of governments and multilateral organizations, including the FCDO, managing nearly $2 billion. Our tailored solutions aim to maximize impact while minimizing risks, ensuring confidence in achieving tangible results.\nAs my colleagues Fumie and Sharon mentioned, they are keen to meet with you and your team covering Sudan and Somalia to explore opportunities for collaboration at your earliest convenience. \nAs promised, I have highlighted examples of Fund Management support that UNOPS has provided for the FCDO. Please also find below a few short stories about some of the projects carried out by UNOPS in Africa. I believe that these initiatives align with your strategic goals in peace and security, climate resilience, health, education, and humanitarian assistance.\nExamples of Fund management support that UNOPS has provided for the FCDO\nStrengthening Humanitarian Preparedness and Response Programme (SHPR)From 2016 - 2021, UNOPS managed the programme: Strengthening Humanitarian Preparedness and Response Programme (SHPR) in Bangladesh. This project received a total funding of $203 million, and provided support to UK-funded actors in Bangladesh. UNOPS performed fund management services, providing oversight, fiduciary management. monitoring and evaluation, as well as the design of an emergency recovery facility. The programme has supported nearly one million Rohingya refugees through scaling up health services, WASH, and education, alongside strengthening the national response to natural disasters.\nModernisation and Improvement of Policing ProjectThe programme aimed to improve security and justice service provision for the poor and traditionally excluded communities in Nepal. The programme received a total funding of $33 million. The objectives of the programme was to change social barriers that impede access to justice, increase engagement among communities and security and justice institutions for better prevention of crime and insecurity and Improved response to criminal violence through improved capabilities and performance of security and justice institutions.\nThe Local Assistance Myanmar Programme (LAMP)This programme is the UK's response in Myanmar through the Humanitarian and Resilience Programme. This programme has so far received a total funding of $61 million The programme was previously managed by Crown Agent. The objective of this programme is for highly vulnerable people in the most conflict affected areas to receive prioritised assistance and protection, primarily from empowered and representative national and Civil Society Organisations (CSOs).The results of the programme include: Delivery of multi-sectoral humanitarian assistance for Rakhine, Shan and Kachin State through provision of grants to CSOs and local or international NGOs (I/NGOs), including capacity building activities for local partners and support to local partner coordination mechanisms.Development of MHAP strategy as part of Programme inceptionEstablishment of framework for monitoring, learning, evidence and knowledge management.\n\nExamples of projects carried out by UNOPS in AfricaPowering peace and sustainable development in West Africa\nOn behalf of Sweden, UNOPS is bringing reliable and sustainable access to clean energy in rural areas of the Liptako-Gourma region of Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger.The project is providing access to clean and sustainable energy for over 300,000 people, and is implemented in collaboration with UNDP and the Liptako-Gourma Authority. UNOPS is responsible for the installation of 6 mini-grids – two in each country – and we are leading the development of local capacity that will ensure that the new systems can be effectively operated and maintained in the longer term. This is a three year pilot project. \nZimbabwe Idai recovery projectThe Zimbabwe Idai recovery was a four-year project, where UNOPS managed a $72 million World Bank fund and worked together with UNICEF, FAO, WHO, WFP, IOM, UNFPA and UNESCO. The project aimed to build the foundations for regional recovery after Cyclone Idai hit Zimbabwe. \nAround 50,000 households were destroyed and more than 60,000 people were displaced. Nine districts faced up to $622 million in damages. The approach of this project was to help the community not just to recover what was lost - but be able to mitigate future shocks. \nThe results of the projects include: Rehabilitation of health facilities, water systems and sanitation facilities, schools, irrigation schemes, micro-watersheds and roads that connected people to services – all infrastructure that's vital to the more than 346,000 people in the impacted communities.Integrated health services and gender-based violence response for more than 672,000 people, including more than 3,600 internally displaced people.Local jobs, thus helping build community capacity and resilience by providing infrastructure development skills. Labor-intensive, community-focused infrastructure projects created job opportunities for more than 5,200 community members, nearly half of them women.Training for local authorities on the operation and maintenance of the infrastructure that was built, ensuring it remains in good working order for communities.Partners involved worked closely with Zimbabwe’s national and local government, to build stronger disaster risk governance and preparedness. This involved developing early warning and risk information systems to inform disaster risk preparedness and contingency planning.To ensure that the project laid the foundation for resilience to future shock and stresses, it involved a comprehensive climate hazard and risk assessment. It made sure to engage the local community’s views via a participatory design process. This approach informed the climate resilient and inclusive design and construction of community schools, roads, health facilities, irrigations and markets.\nReconstruction of social services in YemenFunded by the World Bank, this project restored access to critical services and strengthened resilience to shocks in selected urban cities in Yemen. The project follows an area-based approach for investment in tertiary municipal services, solid waste management, water, sanitation, roads, and electricity. The project targeted approximately 3 million beneficiaries.  UNOPS has designed and rehabilitated critical urban infrastructure damaged by the ongoing conflict, incorporating resilience against identified risks. This includes water, sanitation and solid waste systems, roads and electricity supply. UNOPS has rehabilitated more than 230 kilometers of roads in 8 cities and restored access to critical services for more than 3 million people. A key component of the project aims to boost the local economy and strengthen capacity by partnering with local Yemeni.\nThe results of the projects include: Rehabilitation of over 450 km of urban roads.The enhanced road infrastructure improves road safety, while efficient water drainage systems and newly built retaining walls are designed to protect the road from structural damage and to mitigate the effects of climate change.The upgraded road plays a crucial role in connecting Amran city to Sadah and Hajjah, serving a population of over 200,000 people.To help improve sanitation and avoid health hazards for more than half a million people, UNOPS constructed more than 6 km of new sewage line in Yemen’s historic city of Sana’a\n4. Support to Tunisia’s border control and management of migration flowsFunded by the Italian government, this project aims at supporting Tunisian authorities in the continued and improved control of maritime borders by enhancing operational capacities of the fast patrol fleet of the maritime National Guard.\nWith €15 million funding from the Italian Ministry of Foreign Affairs, UNOPS is implementing this project to provide maintenance services for 6 of the Tunisian Maritime National Guard’s fast patrol boats, as well as the procurement of various equipment for the Tunisian Authorities.\nUNOPS has continuously provided maintenance of 6 patrol boats over a period of two years. This includes: Ordinary maintenance (predictive)Extraordinary maintenance (corrective)Spare parts for six vesselsThe Tunisian authorities’ needs for equipment and supplies were fulfilled; UNOPS has procured and supplied equipment needed by the Tunisian Authorities to a maximum value of €31 million. \nI hope that some of these examples of fund management and projects carried out by UNOPS gives you a good indication of some of the tangible value add UNOPS can provide.\nWe look very much forward to hearing back from you with a view of elaborating what we can offer and hearing more in detail on what you are looking for help with.  \nBest regards, Asbjørn \n-- \n\nAsbjørn Brink| Head of Northern Europe Liaison Office| Partnerships and Liaison Group |HQ |Copenhagen, Denmark| Tel: +45 40 80 36 54 | Follow us on social media and subscribe to our newsletter. | www.unops.org",
