                new (
                    "00TQx00000JSDHFMA5",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Notes from meeting with Finland’s embassy to Ethiopia, 12 December, 2025",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-12-19").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Notes from meeting with Finland’s embassy to Ethiopia, 12 December, 2025",
                        Description = "To: dalilag@unops.org; workneshg@unops.org; petarj@unops.org; elenage@unops.org; amyn@unops.org; mariatew@unops.org\nCC: kirstined@unops.org; emiliep@unops.org; freyavg@unops.org; jakobt@unops.org; asbjornb@unops.org; silkeh@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Notes from meeting with Finland’s embassy to Ethiopia, 12 December, 2025\nBody:\nDear Colleagues,\nOn December 12, Silke, Asbjørn, and I met Pia Korpinen, Education Counsellor at the Embassy of Finland to Ethiopia, and her colleague, Worke.\nMs. Korpinen was only familiar with UNOPS’ capacity for procurement due to a World Bank-funded program supported by Finland. She knew UNOPS as a contractor and for what had happened with S3i in Helsinki. Asbjørn provided an overview of UNOPS’ mandate, capabilities, and the comprehensive and transparent reforms of UNOPS since S3i. \nMs. Korpinen informed that Finland’s country strategy for Ethiopia (2021-2024) had been extended to include 2025. While there would likely be an increased focus on the possibilities of generating bilateral trade, Finland’s development priorities in Ethiopia would likely remain focused on education and WASH.\nSilke outlined potential collaboration opportunities, particularly in education and WASH, highlighting UNOPS’ work in Tigray and areas where Finland could provide support such as school kitchens and WASH systems that could complement work already done by UNOPS. A follow-up meeting in January 2025 was agreed based on more written information about UNOPS’ projects and capacities. \nMs. Korpinen expressed appreciation for the outreach and noted that Finnish colleagues did not have a clear understanding of UNOPS’ very tangible and impactful work. Silke and Asbjørn emphasized UNOPS’ significant presence in Ethiopia, with a portfolio of USD 120-150 million supporting key ministries.\nFollow-up: Silke will provide further information on UNOPS’ WASH projects in Ethiopia and arrange for a follow-up meeting.\n\n                                                                         ***\nOn 12 December 2025 Silke, Asbjørn, and I had the opportunity to meet Pia Korpinen, Education Counsellor at the Embassy of Finland to Ethiopia, who was accompanied by her colleague, Worke.\nMs. Korpinen mentioned her awareness of UNOPS, recognizing its role in procurement and its involvement in a World Bank-funded program supported by Finland.\nAsbjørn provided an overview of UNOPS, including its mandate and capabilities. He also addressed the S3i situation, outlining the comprehensive response plan and current relations between UNOPS and the Finnish government. Ms. Korpinen acknowledged her familiarity with S3i but noticed that it had been handled in a comprehensive and transparent way. \nMs. Korpinen shared insights of Finland’s country strategy for Ethiopia 2021-2024 which had been extended by one year. The process of negotiations with government counterparts was expected to begin in 2025 and Finland is expecting changes within its strategy in 2025 as the strategy has overall been the same for many years. She informed that the strategy would likely shift towards the key guidelines published last year and that we should expect a lighter version in 2026. Given the timing for adjusting the Finnish strategy, it was therefore timely to get introduced to UNOPS’ impactful and tangible work in Ethiopia with a view of considering potential collaboration. \nM.s Korpinen noted that Ville Tavio, Minister for Development and Foreign trade visited Ethiopia in June 2024, highlighting an increased emphasis on trade. However, Finland’s focus areas for development cooperation in Ethiopia are expected to remain unchanged:  \nEducation A World Bank-led sector program (procurement partly outsourced to UNOPS) starting in 2025, with a reduced annual budget from $600M to $200M. Education in emergencies, where UNICEF was one of the key partnersTechnical assistance in education with emphasis on inclusive education Digitalization initiatives, beginning in early 2025 in collaboration with the EU \nWASH - celebrated 30 years of community WASH collaboration in Ethiopia Bilateral collaboration with 7 regionsContribution to the ONEWASH national program.   \nPIF: A programme that was a combination of grants and loans in four regions. The programme had taken a long time to develop and would be a way to try to mobilise private funding via an agreement between Finnish companies, the ministry of finance and the government of Finland.\nSilke noted that there might be synergies in collaborating within education and WASH, due to UNOPS’ extensive work in Tigray - funded by the World Bank. A lot of the ground work, prioritisation of sites, etc. had already been done - which made it possible for partners like Finland to rapidly engage with quick, tangible results. Silke provided concrete examples for opportunities for school kitchen and WASH systems. Silke and Ms. Korpinen agreed that this would be followed up by a meeting in January 2025 based on more written information. \nAsbjørn informed that the World Bank was UNOPS largest partner and that UNOPS was the World Bank's largest implementing partner in fragile countries. Asbjørn further asked how Finland intended to combine trade with development in the Ethiopian context. Pia acknowledged that it is not an easy exercise and that there was no financial gain in the education sector. \nSilke elaborated about UNOPS’ Ethiopia portfolio of about 120-150 million USD and on UNOPS’  support to the ministries of finance, health and agriculture. \nMs. Korpinen informed that many of her colleagues did not have a good understanding of what UNOPS was doing and delivering on behalf of partners like the World Bank - to which Finland provided funding. She informed that Finland collaborated with the same Ministries and that she got little information about the impact of the work funded by Finland. Silke and Asbjørn offered to provide written information on the impact of the work done by UNOPS - to showcase examples of the tangible effects Follow-up: Silke will provide a follow up on UNOPS presence in Ethiopia and work within the WASH and educational sector - as well as written information about UNOPS’ capacities on procurement and infrastructure. \nBest regards, Naimo\n--\nNaimo Hassan Hirsi | Partnerships Analyst | Partnerships and Liaison Group | UNOPS HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Mob: +45 26 83 48 78  | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
                        Location = null,
                        GmailThreadId = null,
                        GmailMessageId = "00TQx00000JSDHFMA5",
