                ),
                new (
                    "00TQx00000JJV8XMAX",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Follow-up on potential collaboration with Novo Nordisk and Novo Nordisk Foundation",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-12-16").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Follow-up on potential collaboration with Novo Nordisk and Novo Nordisk Foundation",
                        Description = "To: COND@novonordisk.com; BDOA@novonordisk.com\nCC: fumiea@unops.org; elenage@unops.org; naimoh@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Follow-up on potential collaboration with Novo Nordisk and Novo Nordisk Foundation\nBody:\nDear Brian and Carolyne,\nIt was such a pleasure meeting with you! Thank you for taking the time to connect with us and share insights into the impactful work you are leading at Novo Nordisk Middle Africa.\nAs promised, I am sharing UNOPS’ health slide deck, which offers an overview of our capabilities in the health sector, along with a factsheet detailing UNOPS’ capacity within this field. \nDuring our discussion, we briefly touched on UNOPS' ongoing partnership with Novo Nordisk A/S. On behalf of Novo Nordisk A/S, we delivered blood transfusion equipment to Ukraine. This equipment supports a station that serves up to 40,000 patients annually. We are currently discussing a new project in Ukraine with your HQ based colleagues on providing medical equipment for several health facilities throughout Ukraine.  \nI understand that challenges such as medicine storage persist in the Sahel region, particularly in Nigeria. Unfortunately, we did not have the opportunity to delve deeper into other challenges you face in the Sahel region. As I briefly mentioned, UNOPS has a strong presence in the region and specializes in operating in difficult and remote areas that are often too difficult to reach.\nIn our meeting, you also noted the synergies between UNOPS and the Novo Nordisk Foundation. While we would be eager to explore collaborative opportunities with the Foundation, we would also like to emphasize our interest in continuing and expanding our collaboration with Novo Nordisk A/S. Both entities offer unique opportunities to drive impactful health outcomes, and we believe there is potential to work together to achieve shared goals.\nAs mentioned, I would be grateful if you could connect us with your relevant colleagues from the Foundation. \nFor reference, please find below a few short stories of some of the projects carried out by UNOPS in Africa. These include examples of our work in the Sahel region, as well as initiatives focused on health. \nLooking forward to hearing your thoughts and continuing our conversation.\nBest regards, Asbjørn\n\nRehabilitating a laboratory in the DRC\nIn collaboration with WHO and with funding from Korea International Cooperation Agency, UNOPS reconstructed the Lubumbashi Provincial Laboratory in DRC. This included reconstructing the building, installing reliable water and electricity supplies, and procuring state-of-the-art laboratory equipment. A fully operational laboratory not only enhances disease detection and medical research but also significantly improves the quality of life for the over 2 million people in Lubumbashi, DRC. Given the country’s pressing health challenges, including measles, yellow fever, chickenpox, and other diseases, transforming this facility into a fully functional laboratory is a vital contribution to the well-being of the population\nResults of the project included: Successful reconstruction of the Lubumbashi Provincial LaboratoryTraining for the local community on the operation and maintenance of the infrastructure that was builtContributed to detecting and combating communicable and noncommunicable diseases  through better diagnostic and research capabilitiesImproved the quality of life for over 2 million residents in Lubumbashi, DRC\nZimbabwe Idai recovery projectThe Zimbabwe Idai recovery  was a four-year project, where UNOPS managed a $72 million World Bank fund and worked together with UNICEF, FAO, WHO, WFP, IOM, UNFPA and UNESCO. The project aimed to build the foundations for regional recovery after Cyclone Idai hit Zimbabwe. \nAround 50,000 households were destroyed and more than 60,000 people were displaced. Nine districts faced up to $622 million in damages. The approach of this project was to help the community not just to recover what was lost - but be able to mitigate future shocks.\nThe results of the project include: rehabilitation of health facilities, water systems and sanitation facilities, schools, irrigation schemes, micro-watersheds and roads that connected people to services – all infrastructure that's vital to the more than 346,000 people in the impacted communities.integrated health services and gender-based violence response for more than 672,000 people, including more than 3,600 internally displaced people.local jobs, thus helping build community capacity and resilience by providing infrastructure development skills. Labor-intensive, community-focused infrastructure projects created job opportunities for more than 5,200 community members, nearly half of them women.training for local authorities on the operation and maintenance of the infrastructure that was built, ensuring it remains in good working order for communities.partners involved worked closely with Zimbabwe’s national and local government, to build stronger disaster risk governance and preparedness. This involved developing early warning and risk information systems to inform disaster risk preparedness and contingency planning.to ensure that the project laid the foundation for resilience to future shock and stresses, it involved a comprehensive climate hazard and risk assessment. It made sure to engage the local community’s views via a participatory design process. This approach informed the climate resilient and inclusive design and construction of community schools, roads, health facilities, irrigations and markets.\nPowering peace and sustainable development in West AfricaOn behalf of Sweden, UNOPS is  currently bringing reliable and sustainable access to clean energy in rural areas of the Liptako-Gourma region of Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger.The project is providing access to clean and sustainable energy for over 300,000 people, and is implemented in collaboration with UNDP and the Liptako-Gourma Authority. UNOPS is responsible for the installation of 6 mini-grids – two in each country – and we are leading the development of local capacity that will ensure that the new systems can be effectively operated and maintained in the longer term. This is a three year pilot project.",
                        Location = null,
                        GmailThreadId = null,
