                new (
                    "00TQx00000HDWjmMAH",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Notes from ED meeting with Permanent Under-Secretary of State at the Danish Prime Minister's Office, Mr. Anders Tang Friborg",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-10-22").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Notes from ED meeting with Permanent Under-Secretary of State at the Danish Prime Minister's Office, Mr. Anders Tang Friborg",
                        Description = "To: eo.cph@unops.org; noa.valentin.katz.sogaard@undp.org; kirstined@unops.org; emiliep@unops.org; banak@unops.org; raady@unops.org; paulom@unops.org; jakobt@unops.org; cilliano@unops.org; marijab@unops.org; naimoh@unops.org\nCC: plg.lo@unops.org; jorge.moreiradasilva@unops.org; annag@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Notes from ED meeting with Permanent Under-Secretary of State at the Danish Prime Minister's Office, Mr. Anders Tang Friborg\nBody:\nDear colleagues,\nOn 21 October the ED met with the Permanent Under-Secretary of State at the Danish Prime Minister's Office, Mr. Anders Tang Friborg and Head of Foreign Affairs Department at the Prime Minister's Office, Mr. Henrik Winther. \nThe ED initially informed about the situation in the Middle East, presenting the Gaza and UNVIM mechanisms as confidence building tools that could be useful to get the facts straight about the aid being delivered. \nUpon information from the ED, the Permanent Under-Secretary of State was positive towards the presented ideas on increased visibility about the tangible work done by the UN and the increased focus on implementation in connection with the CEB in Copenhagen in May 2025 - which would coincide with the Danish membership of the UN Security Council. The Permanent Under-Secretary of State would discuss the idea of a dialogue / fireside chat between the PM and the SG at the UN City in connection with the CEB. The Danish Government would try to utilise their membership of the Security Council to showcase what the UN could do - as the UN's reputation had diminished in recent years. It was therefore important to better show to the taxpayers what the UN could deliver. UNOPS was uniquely situated at this as the implementer of the UN system - headquartered in Denmark.  \nIt was agreed to resume the conversation once a full draft program for the CEB and the potential Helsingør Dialogue had been agreed upon between UNOPS and the Danish MFA. \n***\nThe situation in Gaza and YemenThe ED initially informed about the situation in Gaza and Yemen - explaining the Gaza Mechanism and the Yemen UNVIM as confidence building mechanisms that would enable better decision making based on factual data from the aid entering the conflict areas.  \nThe Permanent Under-Secretary of State afsked if it would be possible to scale the mechanisms for when the reconstruction efforts would start - which the ED confirmed although pointing to the fact that UNOPS could not guarantee the end use of the the products entering Gaza. \nThe Permanent Under-Secretary of State mentioned that he had first encountered UNOPS during his time in Afghanistan. He had therefore followed UNOPS for a number of years. Initially it had been difficult to understand what UNOPS did, the business model, etc. But once he understood it, he had been impressed with UNOPS' ability to deliver tangible results on the ground - especially in difficult places such as Gaza and Afghanistan. \n\nS3iUpon a question from the Permanent Under-Secretary of State, the ED provided information about UNOPS' reform process since the S3i revelations. The Permanent Under-Secretary of State complemented UNOPS for the reform process - which could be a model for other UN agencies hit by crisis. \nUNOPS' mandateThe ED explained UNOPS non-programmatic mandate, focusing on the practical solutions and how the mandate was an enabler to work in special conflict areas such as Afghanistan, Sahel, etc. The Permanent Under-Secretary of State found this approach to be very much in line with Danish priorities and ways of working. \nCEB in Copenhagen and Helsingør DialogueThe ED committed to make the strong UN presence in Copenhagen more known to the broader public as the UN premises were funded by the Danish tax payers. A clear opportunity for this would be the upcoming CEB in Copenhagen in May 2025. The ED would try to maximise visibility which could for example be ensured by a dialogue or fireside chat between the Danish Prime Minister and the UN SG at the UN City back-to-back with the CEB. Such a dialogue could be open to the public/youth as well as the UN personnel based in Copenhagen - potentially also broadcasted publictly. \nThe ED also presented the idea of a Helsingør Dialogue in connection with the CEB focusing on the implementation gap and the need to find practical solutions. Such a focus on the implementation would align well with the Danish pragmatic and solution focused approach. The ED mentioned that he had presented the idea to the Danish MFA State Secretary - offering to invite Denmark as the co-host. \nThe Permanent Under-Secretary of State found the ideas of increased visibility interesting and the scope of focusing on the implementation gap to be fully in line with the Danish approach of making sure that big promises are translated into reality on the ground - finding pragmatic solutions. He committed to discuss the ideas with the PM and flagged that it could also be an opportunity for the PM to point towards the added value of the UN, some of the very tangible results and work done by the UN. This would be a topic for the Danish Membership of the UN Security Council - which would coincide with the CEB in Copenhagen. It might be worthwhile exploiting this. \nThe Permanent Under-Secretary of State pointed out that although the Nordics had always been - and would remain - staunch supporters of the UN, it was clear that the reputation of the UN was diminishing. There was a clear need to showcase to the population what the UN could actually do. This was also a topic that the Danish Prime Minister had discussed with the UN Secretary General during UNGA a few weeks ago. \n\nThe Permanent Under-Secretary of State would await for a draft program for the CEB coordinated between UNOPS and the MFA. Based on this, he would discuss with the Prime Minister. \nFinally the ED offered UNOPS' services and ability to provide objective data on eg. the situation in Gaza and Yemen to the Security Council during the Danish Membership. \n\n/Asbjørn\nAsbjørn Brink | Head of Northern Europe Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | UNOPS HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Mob: +45 40 80 36 54 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
                        Location = null,
                        GmailThreadId = null,
                        GmailMessageId = "00TQx00000HDWjmMAH",
                        EmailAddresses = new List<string> { "paulom@unops.org", "eo.cph@unops.org", "emiliep@unops.org", "kirstined@unops.org", "jorge.moreiradasilva@unops.org", "banak@unops.org", "cilliano@unops.org", "noa.valentin.katz.sogaard@undp.org", "marijab@unops.org", "jakobt@unops.org", "naimoh@unops.org", "annag@unops.org", "plg.lo@unops.org", "raady@unops.org" },
