                    "00TQx00000Gw5B3MAJ",
                    new UNOPSInteraction
                    {
                        Name = "Email: Notes from DED (D&P) meeting with Danish State Secretary for Development Aid, Ms. Lotte Machon",
                        Type = InteractionType.Email,
                        Date = DateTime.Parse("2024-10-15").ToUniversalTime(),
                        Subject = "Email: Notes from DED (D&P) meeting with Danish State Secretary for Development Aid, Ms. Lotte Machon",
                        Description = "To: jorge.moreiradasilva@unops.org; emiliep@unops.org; jakobt@unops.org; eo.cph@unops.org; annag@unops.org; naimoh@unops.org; sonjalk@unops.org; timl@unops.org; simonp@unops.org; anneclaireh@unops.org\nCC: kirstined@unops.org\nBCC: \nAttachment: --none--\n\nSubject: Notes from DED (D&P) meeting with Danish State Secretary for Development Aid, Ms. Lotte Machon\nBody:\nDear colleagues, \nOn October 11 the DED (D&P) met with the State Secretary for Development Aid, Ms. Lotte Machon, at the Danish MFA. \nThe State Secretary complemented the collaboration with UNOPS being very solution oriented, fast and agile - especially in Ukraine where UNOPS were implementing a significant part of the Danish aid. \nDuring the meeting it was agreed to gather 4-5 of the major Danish philanthropic foundations to discuss potential partnerships that could complement some of the Danish government's funded projects in Ukraine - following the format of the recent engagement with Novo Nordisk. \nThe DED informed about the reform process and the upcoming dialogue about UNOPS' future vision and mission. The need to be better at measuring the impact of the work carried out by UNOPS was also discussed in relation to this.  \n***\nThe DED initially informed the State Secretary about the discussions at UNOPS' GLN - where focus had been on the culture shift, partnerships and the future vision and mission for UNOPS. The State Secretary looked forward to an inclusive consultation process on the future vision and mission of UNOPS - and asked about the DED's first impression of UNOPS. \nThe DED explained that the employees at UNOPS were extremely dedicated and very solution oriented. Unlike many other UN entities it was her impression that UNOPS employees were entirely focused on finding practical solutions to the problems at hand. There was however room for improvement in terms of measuring and communicating the quite significant impact of the work done by UNOPS throughout the world. The State Secretary agreed on the need to be better at measuring impact - but also pointed out that the most important part was the actual work on the ground. \nThe State Secretary complemented the collaboration between Denmark and UNOPS - especially in Ukraine where she looked forward to the next 3 year programming period and hoped that UNOPS would continue to play a major role in the implementation of the Danish aid to Ukraine. The Danish Government was also very satisfied with UNOPS' rapid implementation of the Comprehensive Response Plan and looked forward to the independent review so that this chapter could be closed early 2025. \nThe DED informed about the planning of the upcoming CEB in Copenhagen in 2025 and the ongoing reform process - pointing out that the digitalisation reform would take years as more investments had to be done over a number of years. \nThe State Secretary emphasised the Danish Government's policy on more sustainable procurement practices - and desire to make the UN procurement more sustainable. The DED repeated the offer for a dedicated session with the PG on sustainable procurement - and how this could be improved further. The DED also asked the State Secretary to recall the sustainable procurement requirements when asking UNOPS to procure equipment on behalf of Denmark - not only focusing on time and speed. \nUpon a question from the State Secretary it was agreed that the DED and the State Secretary would soon gather 4-5 of the major Danish philanthropic foundations to suggest partnerships that would enhance the effects of some of the Dansh funded projects in especially Ukraine. The approach would build upon the recent experiences with Novo Nordisk, where the government funded the reconstruction of 6 hospitals and Novo Nordisk provided funding for the medical equipment, ambulances, machinery, etc. A similar approach could be done for the schools, where eg. Carlsberg  and LEGO might be interested in funding the school books, IT, furniture etc. NELO and the MFA Ukraine Division would provide a list with suggested projects by week 43 to initiate such a dialogue. \nBest,Asbjørn\nAsbjørn Brink | Head of Northern Europe Liaison Office | Partnerships and Liaison Group | UNOPS HQ | Copenhagen, Denmark | Mob: +45 40 80 36 54 | www.unops.org Keep up-to-date with UNOPS. Follow us on Facebook, LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram, YouTube Subscribe to our external newsletter in English, French or Spanish.",
                        Location = null,
                        GmailThreadId = null,
                        GmailMessageId = "00TQx00000Gw5B3MAJ",
